Ladybird collects normalized option text for innerText on selects and
containers with selects, adding required line breaks around options and
optgroups. Its UA stylesheet gives both elements block display. The
select still paints its labels through its internal shadow tree.

Blink uses the ElementInnerTextCollector::ProcessSelectElement(),
ProcessOptGroupElement(), and ProcessOptionElement() to collect option
text independently of layout objects. WebKit uses HTMLSelectElement::
collectOptionInnerText() from Element::innerText() and TextIterator::
handleReplacedElement(). Both use option text rather than the label
attribute. Ladybird follows that normalized-text behavior, including
options with display:none, instead of synthesizing the normal block
boxes that the rendered-text collection steps describe for options.

Gecko collects text with Range::GetInnerTextNoFlush(), whose helper
IsVisibleAndNotInReplacedElement() allows text inside selects. Its
GetRequiredInnerTextLineBreakCount() adds breaks for block-level frames.
So, Gecko includes the visible option text through its layout frames;
Ladybird collects the text without adding those frames to the control.
All three engines give options and optgroups block display in their UA
stylesheets. Hidden-option handling follows Blink and WebKit here;
Gecko collects text according to frame visibility and rendered text.

Ladybird also checks the insertion target’s editable-subtree flag: A
control can be removed from an editor without permitting insertion
into its contents. Blink’s InsertTextCommand::DoApply() resolves the
visible insertion position, stopping without a usable selection.
WebKit’s editingIgnoresContent() rejects content when its DOM node
cannot contain a range endpoint, as HTMLSelectElement reports via
canContainRangeEndPoint(). Gecko’s HTMLEditUtils identifies controls in
IsNeverElementContentsEditableByUser(), using IsReplacedElement()
to exclude select, optgroup and option contents from editing commands.
